  "problem": "${\u222d}_{R}\u2207\\cdot G\\,\\text{d}V=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We have $\u2207\\cdot G=-4z.$\nFirst, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{\u222d}_{R}\u2207\\cdot G\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}-4z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]\\times [0,4]$ is a rectangular box, and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}-4z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}-2\\cdot 2z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{4}2z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{4}2z\\,\\text{d}z & =-2[x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [y]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{z}^{2}]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (16-0) \\\\  & =-128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: This is equal to the answer found in step 2. This is not a coincidence! It is a consequence of the so-called divergence theorem. If you haven't met the divergence theorem yet, don't worry, you will soon.",
